 Wayfinder is **planning** by default: each ticket resolves a decision, and the map is done when the way is clear, with nothing left to decide before someone goes and does the thing. The pull to just do the work is usually the signal you've reached the edge of the map and it's time to hand off. An effort can override this in its **Notes**, the caller's request, or repo instructions, carrying execution into the map itself. Follow that scope through the chosen skill's completion criteria; absent an execution requirement, produce decisions, not deliverables.

+## Specification destinations
+
+When the **Destination** requires a specification, use `to-spec` for its first draft. Specification publication is required for this destination, as an exception to the planning-only default. While charting, name `to-spec` in **Notes** and in any final specification ticket; such a ticket may use `wayfinder:task` for this handoff.
+
+Start the handoff only when all other on-route tickets are resolved and **Not yet specified** contains no unresolved in-scope fog. Invoke `to-spec` before drafting, using the map as its source. If the host has no skill invocation tool, read the installed `to-spec/SKILL.md` in full and follow it. Complete that skill's process and template through publication. If the skill is unavailable, stop before drafting and tell the user how to invoke it. A substitute document does not satisfy the destination.
+
+Keep any specification ticket open until `to-spec` has published the specification. Then record the specification link on the map and resolve that ticket through the usual resolution steps. The run ends at the published specification.
+
 ## Refer by name

 Every map and ticket is an issue, so it has a **name**: its title. In everything the human reads (narration, the map's Decisions-so-far), refer to it by that name, never by a bare id, number, or slug. A wall of `#42, #43, #44` is illegible; names read at a glance. The id and URL don't vanish; a name wraps its link, but they ride _inside_ the name, never stand in for it.
